That's not the correct framing in IMO because Fed usually treats surges in crude prices (or other supply shocks) as one-time events.  That's not the case this time because we have had too many one-time events since COVID and Fed needs to respond to actual (not core) inflation misses

@geswolfcrest Simpler: There have been times when interest rates would fall when crude prices surged.  Not this time

I thought this was obvious but maybe not: this is definitely not the time for the Fed to be too nuanced in their view of inflation.  With inflation having missed their target for years, they need overall inflation (including energy) to come back down to target. That's why crude prices and UST yields have been joined at the hip recently and short duration inflation swaps are meaningful to their decision making

